WEBVTT

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:04.190
In this place so unreal

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:07.820
I wait for dawn to come

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.950
Farther and farther, like the sky I saw that day

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:19.240
What a joke this hope is

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:22.750
I acted as if I had forgotten

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:28.250
Farther and farther, like the dream I saw that day

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.130
I just want to know the meaning of it

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.300
Within this scattered time

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:41.140
Along with the breath of ours

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:45.060
And of this pain that never goes away

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:48.610
Even though the roaring of the sea muffles the voices

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.530
Darkness engulfs everything ahead

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:56.530
The light that passes through our fragile hearts

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:59.950
Won't go away

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:03.790
We only relied on our compass

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.920
We believed you

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:14.760
While we stand here

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:19.930
We're searching for the unseen future

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:29.860
Zipang

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:33.900
It's important that a ship's crew is well rested.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.450
And a homeport is necessary for maintenance and re-supply.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.990
You're suggesting Truk as our new homeport?

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.490
- Yokosuka.
- What?

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.700
That is your homeport, isn't it?

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:49.830
Reunion

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.470
The assault on Guadalcanal is back on?! But why?

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:00.260
Even the commander-in-chief can't go against orders from Central Command.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:04.470
Then, what about Lieutenant Okamura and his stranded engineers?!

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:06.640
Just like in history,

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.730
they will have to fight in the battle alongside Ichiki's men.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:14.440
Where is our friend Kusaka now?

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:18.910
Admiral Yamamoto would like to keep him in Truk for a bit.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:23.830
Captain, I have one request.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:38.880
That welcome party was fantastic! All the women were babes.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.260
After two months cooped up on this ship,

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:43.810
you've lowered your standards a little, huh?

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:46.100
- But it's true!
- H-Hey!

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:54.520
That was close. I thought he was going to tear us a new one...

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:57.400
Lieutenant Tsuda. Please come with me.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:00.740
The Captain has granted permission for you to go ashore.

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:02.740
Yes, gladly.

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:07.200
I need to have a chat with Lieutenant Kusaka as well.

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:15.090
I'm going ashore now.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.000
Come back soon.

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:19.920
Decisions made by the Central Command

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.010
are officially sanctioned by the Emperor.

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:25.970
Not following those orders is like rebelling against the Emperor.

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:29.020
That's the tactic Tsuji is using.

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:32.310
I'm sure Admiral Yamamoto must be all worked up over the decision.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:34.110
All worked up?

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:35.980
Upon Kusaka's advice,

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:38.570
he promised to halt the Guadalcanal invasion,

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:42.200
while sending in the rest of the fleet to shell the red beach,

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:44.240
and wipe out all the US Marines on shore.

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:47.290
If that's not an assault on Guadalcanal, what is?!

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:50.290
Kusaka's the one behind all this.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.430
Kusaka!!

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:06.180
I've found you at last! Lieutenant Tsuda, let's go!

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:07.760
Yes, sir!

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:09.850
XO! Where're you going?!

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:11.770
Go on ahead without me!

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:13.640
Too bad for you, Kusaka!

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:16.360
You can run but you won't get off this island!

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:22.030
Acquaintances of yours?

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:24.280
He saved my life once.

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:28.530
And, he's an important... friend.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:31.620
In that case, should we stop the car?

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.000
No. I'm sure I'll be seeing him again shortly.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.420
Keep going.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:41.920
He was in that car with a damn Army Officer!

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:45.680
Where the hell is he going?!

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:48.810
To the jetty! There's a shortcut here!

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:06.700
No matter where you run to, you won't get away from me!

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:11.290
We have to hurry. That boat's headed for Takeshima!

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:15.000
Takeshima? The airstrip...

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.670
- Why not?!
- I can't do that, Lieutenant!

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:23.510
This ship is assigned to the Hiei's Captain, Commander Nishida!

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:26.720
That doesn't matter! We have orders from Admiral Yamamoto!

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:27.970
Yes, sir!

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:39.560
There are no more scheduled flights for today.

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:43.230
- Are you sure?!
- Yes, and that reminds me.

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:46.400
We just had an emergency flight arranged to carry Officer Tsuji.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:48.950
- Officer Tsuji?!
- That's it!

00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:50.950
Where are they?!

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:07.590
It's them... in that Type-100 Scout!

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:11.760
Kusaka! Stop him, Tsuda!

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:15.600
That's an Army plane. I don't have the authority to stop it.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.270
Who cares about authority now?! Isn't this the Imperial Naval base?!

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:22.770
If you're not gonna stop it, I will!

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:33.030
Hold it right there!!

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:34.540
What should I do?

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:36.790
- Just take off.
- Roger.

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:40.540
Running away, Takumi?! You idiot!

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:43.290
No matter where you go, or where you hide,

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:46.090
I will definitely hunt you down!

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:51.890
What's that?! I can't hear you! What are you saying?!

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:57.980
Runway clear!

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:01.900
You lying bastard!

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:04.730
Even if you fool everyone else, you can never fool me!

00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:06.820
I'll find you!!

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:27.510
Ishiwara...

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:29.970
You're going to meet Ishiwara?

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:35.100
You mean, General Ishiwara Kanji?

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:39.930
30 degrees left. Two ships spotted.

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:42.270
Those two transport ships belong to the army.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:45.440
I think they're headed for the Truk base.

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:48.690
I think Ichiki's unit is onboard those transport ships.

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:51.780
After they arrive in Truk, they should be heading for Guadalcanal next.

00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:55.320
No, they are going to hell.

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:57.790
Can you descend to water level?

00:07:57.790 --> 00:07:59.580
Roger!

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:11.670
Hey! It's a friendly plane!

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:15.220
They're here to cheer us on!

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:19.100
We are the strongest Army unit, the Ichiki Unit!

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:21.890
We'll take back Guadalcanal! Just watch us!

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:24.730
We'll slaughter those marines in 3 days!

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:28.070
Shall we respond with a banking maneuver, sir?

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:30.780
No, there's no need to.

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:42.200
I will not let these two thousand lives be lost in vain.

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:46.670
Thank you. Let's be on our way.

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:47.750
Roger.

00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:59.760
General Ishiwara was born in Yamagata in 1889.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:04.480
He graduated from Military Academy, and then was stationed in Germany.

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:06.850
As the staff officer of the Kanto Army,

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:10.270
he played a central role in the Manchurian Incident.

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:13.780
The Manchurian Incident started the invasion of China!

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:17.950
It says here that his thinking and foresight were way ahead of his time,

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:20.030
and in his book, The Theory of the Final War,

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:21.620
he accurately predicted the advancement of weaponry

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:24.080
which revolutionized the way wars are fought.

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:27.710
The most shocking thing was, he even predicted nuclear weapons,

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:31.630
things that changed all military strategy in the late 20th century.

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:34.300
He looks too smart for his own good.

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:37.090
His views didn't make him many friends.

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:41.390
He advocated equality for all, was against the invasion of China,

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:43.640
and opposed the war with the Americans.

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:47.440
He even criticized the head of the Army, Tojo Hideki.

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:49.810
After that, he was dismissed from the army.

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:51.770
He's a dangerous man.

00:09:51.770 --> 00:09:54.530
He possesses superb tactical knowledge and insight.

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:56.820
His modern theories also garnered many followers.

00:09:57.200 --> 00:09:59.120
He's the reason why many Army Officers

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:01.490
have started making their own important decisions.

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:05.200
But I still don't understand.

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:08.330
If Kusaka has set out to change the course of this war,

00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:11.090
why doesn't he approach Prime Minister Tojo Hideki instead?

00:10:11.090 --> 00:10:13.050
Ishiwara may be an important figure,

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:16.590
but now he's nothing but a disgraced, old soldier.

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:19.340
Compared to Tojo's authority, he is powerless.

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:22.680
Even within the army, he's already a figure of the past.

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:24.430
That may be so in Japan,

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:27.100
but he may still have some important connections in China.

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:30.400
China? You mean, besides influencing the army,

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:33.860
Kusaka may be plotting something even bigger?

00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:36.740
If not... then maybe he's looking for a spokesman.

00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:38.990
Spokesman? What do you mean?

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:42.620
Kusaka is actually pretty shy in front of other people.

00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:48.660
Commander, Lieutenant, the Captain has called a meeting.

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:51.000
Please report to the Captain's Quarters.

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:57.550
I personally think we should accept the proposal...

00:10:57.550 --> 00:11:00.890
...that Admiral Yamamoto has laid on the table for us.

00:11:00.890 --> 00:11:02.640
We're going back to Yokosuka?!

00:11:02.890 --> 00:11:05.220
However, a return to the port...

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:08.560
...does not imply that we are falling under the combined fleet's command.

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:12.860
This is just a personal suggestion by Admiral Yamamoto himself.

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:16.400
The Mirai will continue to operate autonomously.

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:21.860
For the past two months, we've been subconsciously deluding ourselves.

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:24.120
We've been trying to convince ourselves...

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:26.870
...that this world isn't real and we'll return to our world at any moment.

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:30.660
But, we have to stop running away.

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:33.460
The time has come for us to face the truth.

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:35.630
What do you think, Weapons Officer?

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:41.470
If the safety of the crew members is completely assured then...

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:45.720
And on the way to Yokosuka, we may just get lucky

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:48.470
and encounter the same storm that sent us back in time and go home!

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:51.980
Thank you. XO, what do you think?

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:55.940
Sir, just as you trust Admiral Yamamoto,

00:11:55.940 --> 00:11:58.780
I put my trust in you too, Captain.

00:12:05.990 --> 00:12:10.500
The Imperial Army has high expectations from all of you!

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:12.370
The recapture of Guadalcanal Island

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:15.080
has become the responsibility of the Army!

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:16.960
Because of the importance of this operation,

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:22.510
I, Tsuji Masanobu, will join all of you in going ashore at Guadalcanal,

00:12:22.510 --> 00:12:25.010
and I will be leading all of you personally!

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:27.760
I heard the American soldiers are even weaker than the Chinese,

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:29.430
so we've got nothing to fear!

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:30.930
In the face of our superior will,

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:33.190
the Americans will be running away before the battle begins!

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:35.730
The Staff Officer is full of spirit,

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:37.940
but he knows nothing about the real situation.

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:41.190
The enemy is fully equipped with vehicles and weapons.

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:43.360
Well, all we need to worry about...

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:47.200
is rescuing Lieutenant Okamura and then getting out of there.

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:50.490
We will wipe out all the American Troops!

00:12:53.330 --> 00:12:56.000
A ship from 60 years into the future.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:00.460
So it's a mystery ship... from the Showa Period?

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:06.970
I caught a glimpse of it this morning. It does look quite different.

00:13:06.970 --> 00:13:11.680
Somehow, Tsuji that sewer rat from the army also noticed it.

00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:13.890
Of course, I've already destroyed the letter...

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:16.810
that he has sent back to Central Command.

00:13:16.940 --> 00:13:20.020
In three years' time, Japan surrenders unconditionally,

00:13:20.020 --> 00:13:22.070
and the Imperial Navy is destroyed.

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:26.410
If we allow a ship bearing such dark tidings into the Yokosuka Base,

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:28.780
what then will happen to Japan?

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:32.040
Admiral, I'm sure you understand what I'm getting at.

00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:33.870
I fear that Admiral Yamamoto...

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:36.460
...may have gone a little senile from the excessive exposure...

00:13:36.460 --> 00:13:39.170
...to the sea breeze here at Truk.

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:42.590
Lieutenant Taki.

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:45.920
You shall not speak that way about the Admiral!

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:49.680
Are you taking orders from Chief Oka of the Military Affairs Bureau?

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:51.810
Or perhaps it's from Minister Shimada?

00:13:51.810 --> 00:13:55.180
I work for the interests of the Imperial Navy.

00:13:55.180 --> 00:13:57.810
That is all I can tell you at this point in time.

00:13:59.860 --> 00:14:03.070
What are you planning to do with that ship?

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:07.320
The distance from Truk to Yokosuka is 14900 miles,

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:09.030
anything can happen.

00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:16.910
You have not witnessed the ship's full capabilities,

00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:18.750
that's why you're making such claims.

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:24.550
Even the Yamato herself may not be able to win a battle against it.

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:26.170
To sink that ship,

00:14:26.170 --> 00:14:29.260
you might even need to use every ship in the whole combined fleet.

00:14:32.390 --> 00:14:33.760
Yokosuka?!

00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:39.190
We can go home!

00:14:39.190 --> 00:14:42.110
Even if it's the Yokosuka of the past,

00:14:42.110 --> 00:14:44.570
it's still our homeport!

00:14:48.650 --> 00:14:52.740
Have you forgotten about our other opponent?

00:14:54.410 --> 00:14:57.410
I'm talking about the American Force.

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:05.130
Ritsumeikan University

00:15:05.590 --> 00:15:08.840
At present, Japan is headed for a defeat.

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:14.850
Theory of
National Defense
- Ishiwara Kanji

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:14.850
Theory of
National Defense
- Ishiwara Kanji

00:15:10.090 --> 00:15:16.220
Right now, Japan is trying to buy things with only 100 yen in her pocket.

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:21.850
While America is trying to buy the same things with a 1 million yen note.

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:25.650
We're getting drained slowly and we don't yet feel the pinch,

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:27.900
but with the Americans' superior resources,

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:30.070
there'll be nothing left but bankruptcy.

00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:33.740
Since the Sino-Japanese War, Japan has been expanding out of control.

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:36.160
Without caution or foresight,

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:38.370
she's behaving like a bankrupt company...

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:40.790
...trying to expand beyond its means to do so.

00:15:41.250 --> 00:15:43.130
Tojo's Army Faction expanded into the South

00:15:43.130 --> 00:15:45.250
because they wanted the oil resources.

00:15:45.250 --> 00:15:47.050
But the Imperial Navy...

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:50.220
doesn't have a strong defensive perimeter to hold the territory.

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:52.010
If we spread out our military resources

00:15:52.010 --> 00:15:53.970
to defend on both a Northern and Southern front,

00:15:54.050 --> 00:15:56.930
there will be no one left to defend our homeland!

00:15:57.970 --> 00:16:00.770
If this keeps up, there'll be no ships left in Japan,

00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:02.690
and the country of Japan...

00:16:03.020 --> 00:16:07.230
will become a barren field just like my head!

00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:10.280
Stop the lecture!

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:13.740
You have crossed the line today!

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:18.700
Impudent fool! If you don't understand my lecture, just leave this room!

00:16:18.700 --> 00:16:21.580
- What?!
- I see, you're a soldier.

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:25.830
If you're a soldier, you should study the military theories in my books.

00:16:25.830 --> 00:16:29.210
If you refuse to leave the room, then keep quiet and listen.

00:16:30.050 --> 00:16:34.050
Well, the formations used in war have evolved through history.

00:16:34.050 --> 00:16:37.970
From the Square to the Line, to the Skirmish Line formations,

00:16:37.970 --> 00:16:40.850
now we also have the Battle County.

00:16:40.850 --> 00:16:43.440
Let me explain this using geometry.

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:46.360
First, we have the Square as the base,

00:16:47.110 --> 00:16:49.980
then the ranks in front form the Line.

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:52.150
And then we have the Skirmish Line further ahead.

00:16:52.150 --> 00:16:55.200
The Battle County is what supplies the resources to all of them.

00:16:55.200 --> 00:16:59.120
Nowadays, the presence of aerial warfare has presented us with...

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:00.910
...a three-dimensional battlefield.

00:17:00.910 --> 00:17:03.410
In short, it has made things more complex.

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:07.130
Air attacks and defense have become very important, that is to say,

00:17:07.130 --> 00:17:10.460
domination of the skies is now the key to success in a long war.

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:15.930
This is the strategic situation that we are currently facing.

00:17:16.760 --> 00:17:19.350
Now, what about a fourth dimension?

00:17:19.350 --> 00:17:20.970
If such a dimension exists,

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:24.140
then according to Einstein, that's a time and place not of this world.

00:17:26.270 --> 00:17:30.980
Unfortunately, that's not a dimension that we humans can control.

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:35.360
If anyone claims to have any control over it, I'd love to meet him.

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:37.530
I have seen it.

00:17:37.530 --> 00:17:42.120
Mr. Ishiwara, your powers of predicting the future are impressive,

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:44.370
but you are no match for me.

00:17:44.370 --> 00:17:47.670
I have personally experienced the world of the future.

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:53.380
- What's that guy talking about?
- Is he crazy?

00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:57.260
Looking forward in time is normally impossible, of course.

00:17:57.260 --> 00:18:00.930
But I've recently been presented with the future course of this war.

00:18:12.610 --> 00:18:15.320
The ultimate weapon that you have predicted...

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:19.160
...will be completed by the Americans in 2 years and 11 months time.

00:18:19.160 --> 00:18:21.030
Just as you have prophesized,

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:25.500
the use of this ultimate weapon on civilian targets will end this war.

00:18:25.790 --> 00:18:29.790
I thought it would be the Germans to develop it first.

00:18:29.790 --> 00:18:32.960
Hitler feels that it wouldn't be ready in time for this war,

00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:35.050
and has stopped the research project.

00:18:35.050 --> 00:18:39.180
Italy and Germany will surrender before its completion.

00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:42.300
So the first ones to experience its effects will be...

00:18:47.680 --> 00:18:50.650
This is unusual for a Japanese soldier.

00:18:51.610 --> 00:18:53.570
You're serious about this, right?

00:18:53.570 --> 00:18:56.110
That's enough! This lecture is over!

00:18:56.110 --> 00:18:58.280
Hey! You're a suspicious fella.

00:18:58.280 --> 00:19:00.660
Come with me! I want to ask you some questions.

00:19:00.660 --> 00:19:02.320
So the lecture is over?

00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:03.780
Of course it is.

00:19:03.780 --> 00:19:07.950
A lecture criticizing the military isn't going to be of any help.

00:19:07.950 --> 00:19:09.410
If you're going to stop my lecture...

00:19:09.410 --> 00:19:12.040
...because you think it's not of any help to this country,

00:19:12.290 --> 00:19:16.000
then maybe you could give a talk that's going to be of some help?

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:17.510
W-What did you say?!

00:19:17.510 --> 00:19:20.550
The podium is now empty. Don't be shy.

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:23.550
Go and speak in my place.

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:28.480
If you say the professor's talk is useless, then you give us a talk then!

00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:31.020
You're not going to weasel out of this!

00:19:31.020 --> 00:19:33.650
You speak instead of the professor!

00:19:33.650 --> 00:19:35.610
That's right! Go on! Hurry up!

00:19:35.610 --> 00:19:40.070
Go on and tell us something useful if you are so smart!

00:19:39.490 --> 00:19:42.490
Theory of
National Defense

00:19:46.280 --> 00:19:48.950
By the way, may I ask for your name?

00:19:48.950 --> 00:19:53.000
Yes, former Navy Lieutenant Commander, Takumi Kusaka.

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:56.500
So you're an outcast like me too.

00:19:56.500 --> 00:19:58.000
By the way, Mr. Kusaka...

00:19:58.300 --> 00:20:01.880
You said you experienced the future, is this true?

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:04.430
I was on board a certain ship.

00:20:11.060 --> 00:20:14.810
And so... why have you come to see me then?

00:20:15.400 --> 00:20:19.030
If you are looking for the guy in charge, Tojo's the man.

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:22.900
That man is... too weak.

00:20:22.900 --> 00:20:25.990
But he's been killing off all his enemies and allies.

00:20:25.990 --> 00:20:27.950
It's possible to kill off millions of your own people

00:20:27.950 --> 00:20:30.200
without having to see their faces.

00:20:30.540 --> 00:20:33.420
But you... To achieve your goals,

00:20:33.420 --> 00:20:38.040
you'd betray your benefactors, and sacrifice your trusted subordinates.

00:20:38.040 --> 00:20:41.550
Even your treasured army... can be defeated.

00:20:49.060 --> 00:20:50.890
You overestimate me.

00:20:50.890 --> 00:20:55.190
I'm just a powerless old soldier stuck on reserve duty.

00:20:55.520 --> 00:20:59.110
Not too long ago, the Japan of 60 years into the future...

00:20:59.110 --> 00:21:01.780
appeared in front of my eyes.

00:21:08.580 --> 00:21:12.120
Hawaii      Pearl Harbor

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:17.830
Lieutenant!

00:21:18.380 --> 00:21:19.420
What is it?

00:21:19.420 --> 00:21:22.630
I noticed something in the recent telegrams sent by...

00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:24.510
...the Japanese Navy at Truk.

00:21:24.590 --> 00:21:27.640
The code CX keeps coming up.

00:21:27.640 --> 00:21:31.350
From the context of the message, I think it's a ship's name.

00:21:31.350 --> 00:21:34.850
CX? I've never heard them use that code before.

00:21:34.850 --> 00:21:37.310
- Is it a new ship?
- The interesting thing is...

00:21:37.310 --> 00:21:39.820
This CX always appears together...

00:21:39.820 --> 00:21:43.240
in the same messages with the codes for Yamamoto and Guadalcanal.

00:21:43.490 --> 00:21:45.740
It must be an important ship.

00:21:45.740 --> 00:21:49.530
Lieutenant! We've just deciphered a new telegram!

00:21:49.530 --> 00:21:52.990
CX will depart from Truk at 1800 today!

00:21:53.540 --> 00:21:57.000
It's scheduled for docking at Yokosuka in one week.

00:21:57.000 --> 00:21:59.840
All units are to be on stand by.

00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:03.670
Inform Admiral Nimitz right away!

00:22:09.720 --> 00:22:17.200
Passing through the distant time

00:22:17.330 --> 00:22:22.440
Just peacefully

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:34.250
Without losing your way, you walked towards me

00:22:35.380 --> 00:22:47.130
How many times have I grasped tightly those small hands of yours?

00:22:47.630 --> 00:22:53.730
Not being able to do anything

00:22:54.300 --> 00:22:59.810
I kept on praying

00:23:01.210 --> 00:23:11.650
Sorrow or agony isn't enough

00:23:13.520 --> 00:23:27.340
That's why now as if I wanted to make up... I'm looking at you

00:23:40.840 --> 00:23:43.890
Has Admiral Yamamoto decided to give up on Guadalcanal,

00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:46.640
and retreat to the South Pacific instead?

00:23:47.140 --> 00:23:48.850
This is proof of it.

00:23:48.850 --> 00:23:51.900
History is not created by chance, but by human beings themselves.

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:57.360
In my opinion, I'd say without the Navy, there's no Japan.

00:23:57.360 --> 00:23:59.780
When the time comes, we'll have to take them down...

00:23:59.780 --> 00:24:01.780
before they can make a report with standard Anti-Air Missiles.

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:04.950
Next Episode: The Other Staff Headquarters

00:24:02.120 --> 00:24:04.700
The answer lies in the North.

00:24:04.950 --> 00:24:09.960
This story is purely fiction. The names of the characters and organizations that appear have no connections with actual people and organizations.
